1: ## P3K.23. Proved: the signed change under the actual fixed-edge flop
2: 
3: Consider one of the actual sphere families constructed above with a
4: semifree fixed curve \(F\simeq\mathbb P^1\), whose complex normal bundle
5: is \(\mathcal O(-1)\oplus\mathcal O(-1)\). Perform the toric flop of
6: this fixed edge in the periodic cusp model, retaining the actual
7: parameter clutch and all exterior gluings. Write the original positive
8: return's Jin index as
9: \[
10:              A=\frac{4k-\ell}{24},
11: \]
12: The positive return of the flopped family has oriented class
13: \[
14:                          -5A\pmod {28}                 \tag{K23.1}
15: \]
16: in the run's normalization of \(\Theta _7\). The flopped curve has two
17: isolated circle fixed points. Formula (K23.1) specifies its homotopy
18: sphere class; it does not assign a new Jin integer to that circle
19: action.
20: 
21: In particular, the four groups of primitive-source examples in
22: P3K.22 give the following flopped classes:
23: 
24: | \(d=1/h(G)\) | Original index \(A\) | Flopped class in \(\mathbb Z/28\) |
25: |---|---|---|
26: | \(8\) | \(-3\) | \(15\) |
27: | \(14\) | \(-6\) | \(2\) |
28: | \(20\) | \(-9\) | \(17\) |
29: | \(30\) | \(-14\) | \(14\) |
30: 
31: The earlier degree-four OS47 example of index \(-4\) gives class
32: \(20\). Every original zero-index example remains class zero after
33: this fixed-edge flop. No formula for a flop at another toric edge is
34: asserted here.
35: 
36: **Proof: the actual two local products and the parameter clutch.**
37: Write the conifold link as
38: \[
39:  K=(S^3_a\times S^3_c)/S^1,\qquad
40:  (a,c)\sim(e^{i\phi}a,e^{-i\phi}c).
41: \]
42: With \(\mathbb H\) oriented by \((1,i,j,k)\), put
43: \[
44:  u=a_0+j a_1,\quad v=c_0+j\overline{c_1},\quad
45:  q=uv,\quad h=u i u^{-1},\quad k=v^{-1} i v.
46:                                                         \tag{K23.2}
47: \]
48: The first resolution has the actual product coordinates \((h,q)\)
49: on its real normal sphere bundle; the second has \((k,q)\).
50: To invert the first map, choose a unit representative \(u\) of \(h\)
51: and put \(v=u^{-1}q\). Changing the representative changes only the
52: displayed auxiliary-circle orbit. For the second choose \(v\) and
53: put \(u=qv^{-1}\). These inverse formulas are smooth in the standard
54: quotient charts. The common boundary map is consequently
55: \[
56:              \tau(h,q)=(q^{-1}h q,q).                  \tag{K23.3}
57: \]
58: The fixed sphere is unknotted in the smooth six-sphere:
59: Haefliger's Theorem 1.2 and Corollary 6.6, with \(n=2,q=4\),
60: give the trivial embedding class \cite{Haefliger1966}.
61: Its exterior is therefore the actual product
62: \(E=D^3_y\times S^3_q\), with its given boundary marking extended
63: by the normal framing. The map (K23.3) extends over this exterior:
64: \[
65:  T(y,q)=(q^{-1}yq,q),\qquad
66:  T^{-1}(y,q)=(q yq^{-1},q).                           \tag{K23.4}
67: \]
68: These are smooth also at \(y=0\). Thus replacing the local resolution
69: conjugates the exterior return by \(T\).
70: 
71: This last sentence includes the actual parameter family. For a
72: local Cox angular action of weights
73: \((-r-s,0,r,s)\), write
74: \[
75:  a_\theta=e^{-i(r+s)\theta/2},\qquad
76:  b_\theta=e^{i(r-s)\theta/2}.
77: \]
78: The first product action and second product action are respectively
79: \[
80:  (h,q)\longmapsto
81:  (a_\theta h a_\theta^{-1},a_\theta q b_\theta),\qquad
82:  (k,q)\longmapsto
83:  (b_\theta^{-1}k b_\theta,a_\theta q b_\theta).
84: \]
85: Substitution in (K23.3) proves exact equivariance for every \(\theta\).
86: At \(2\pi\), both scalar endpoints have the same central sign;
87: the displayed product actions close even when \(r+s\) is odd.
88: Therefore the comparison of the two local family products is the
89: same map \(\tau\), independent of the parameter angle. The period
90: reindexing acts on the periodic collection of common conifold cells.
91: That collection has trivial cell stabilizer; choose one quotient
92: representative and extend every chart change equivariantly to its
93: translates. Extracting the integer winding from each bounded
94: nonzero toric unit leaves a logarithm on this representative. Contract
95: its positive modulus and its remaining logarithm in both resolution
96: charts at once, transporting the truncation collars by the resulting
97: common conifold isotopy. The integer winding is exactly the Cox
98: action just displayed. This retains the actual exterior
99: identifications, including their bounded units. No null-homotopy of
100: an odd Cox loop, or additional disc-supported correction, is inserted.
101: 
102: **Proof: exact relative generators and conjugation.**
103: Choose a smooth degree-one map
104: \(\zeta:(D^3,S^2)\to(S^3,1)\), constant on a boundary collar,
105: which is equivariant for quaternionic conjugation and commutes
106: with \(y\). Explicitly one can use
107: \[
108:  \zeta(y)=\cos\phi(|y|)-\sin\phi(|y|)\frac{y}{|y|},
109: \]
110: where \(\phi\) decreases from \(\pi\) to zero, has the smooth odd
111: radial expansion at zero, and is flat near the boundary. Define
112: \[
113:  L(y,q)=(y,\zeta(y)q),\qquad R(y,q)=(y,q\zeta(y)).
114:                                                         \tag{K23.5}
115: \]
116: They commute and extend over \(D^3\times D^4\) by the same quaternion
117: multiplications. Their induced six-sphere classes are therefore zero.
118: They represent the left and right generators of
119: \(\pi_3SO(4)=\mathbb Z^2\). Extension by the identity to the double
120: injects the relative pseudo-isotopy group into the doubled group;
121: Krylov's Proposition 1 and Lemma 3 give, in this dimension, the
122: relative group \(\Theta _7\oplus\mathbb Z^2\)
123: \cite{Krylov2004}. Pseudo-isotopy is sufficient here, since extending
124: the resulting collar gives the same oriented sphere gluing.
125: 
126: On the double write
127: \[
128:  {\cal A}(s,t)=(tst^{-1},t),\quad
129:  {\cal B}(s,t)=(s,sts^{-1}),\quad
130:  {\cal C}=[{\cal A},{\cal B}].
131: \]
132: Krylov's Theorems 2--3 establish centrality and order \(28\) of
133: \({\cal C}\), and the relation
134: \({\cal A}Y=Y{\cal A}{\cal B}\), where \(Y(s,t)=(s,st)\)
135: \cite{Krylov2001}. In the marked relative products
136: \[
137:        T={\cal A}^{-1},\qquad L=Y,\qquad
138:        R=Y{\cal B}^{-1}.
139: \]
140: The first identity below also follows by literal substitution in
141: (K23.4)--(K23.5); the second then follows from the displayed central
142: relation:
143: \[
144:  TLT^{-1}=R,\qquad
145:  TRT^{-1}=L^{-1}R^2{\cal C}.
146: \]
147: Consequently
148: \[
149:  T(L^mR^n{\cal C}^s)T^{-1}
150:        =L^{-n}R^{m+2n}{\cal C}^{s+n}.                \tag{K23.6}
151: \]
152: All maps on the left are relative to the boundary. The injection
153: just cited retains this equality in the relative group. Transport
154: of a supporting oriented disc shows that \(T\) fixes the central
155: disc-supported subgroup.
156: 
157: **Proof: the actual integer coefficient from the Hopf quotient.**
158: The orientations in (K23.2) are essential. At \(a_1=x+iy=0\),
159: the derivatives of \(h\) are \((-2k,-2j)\), so \(h\) reverses the
160: complex curve orientation. At \(u=1\),
161: \[
162:  q=\Re c_0+i\Im c_0+j\Re c_1+k\Im c_1
163: \]
164: preserves the complex normal orientation. Thus the complex
165: orientation of the exterior is \(D^3_y\times S^3_q\).
166: For the second resolution the derivatives of \(k\) are \((2k,-2j)\);
167: its base preserves and its normal coordinate reverses the respective
168: complex orientations. Its exterior has the same product orientation.
169: 
170: The physical delta action is \(q\mapsto q e^{-i\theta}\), with
171: Hopf quotient \(z=q i q^{-1}\). At \(q=1\), its circle vector is
172: \(-i\), and the quotient derivatives in the \(j,k\) directions are
173: \(-2k,2j\). Hence
174: \[
175:  [S^3_q]=-[S^1_\delta][S^2_z],\qquad
176:  [E]=[S^1_\delta][D^3_y][S^2_z].                    \tag{K23.7}
177: \]
178: The closed conifold quotient therefore has orientation
179: \(S^3_y\times S^2_z\), equivalently \(S^2_z\times S^3_y\).
180: 
181: Let \(t\) be the relative variation of the actual exterior return in
182: this product. The integral circle Gysin argument can be made
183: explicit. Put \(P=Y\setminus\operatorname{int}\nu F\), the orbit
184: complement before its conifold cap. On its physical mapping torus,
185: whose fourth cohomology vanishes, let \(\eta\)
186: be a circle connection with \(d\eta=x\), and take
187: \(d\beta=x^2\). The integral degree-three generator of the circle
188: total space is represented by
189: \(-\eta x+\pi^*\beta\); fibre integration and the meridian-one
190: condition fix its sign. Its boundary restriction in the complex
191: curve orientation is \(s+k_{\rm Jin}h_F\theta\):
192: Stokes gives \(k_{\rm phys}=-\int_{F\times S^1}\beta\), and the
193: physical mapping torus is the inverse of Jin's convention.
194: Since \(h=-h_F\), its restriction in (K23.2) is
195: \[
196:                          t=-k_{\rm Jin}.             \tag{K23.8}
197: \]
198: Equivalently, the boundary of a relative \(D^3\times[0,1]\) is
199: \(S^2\times[0,1]-D^3\times\{1\}+D^3\times\{0\}\), giving precisely
200: this variation coefficient. The integral Gysin sequence supplies
201: the integral class; choosing differential representatives does not
202: replace the integral assertion.
203: 
204: The generator \(L\) has variation \(+1\) and quotient
205: \((y,z)\mapsto(y,\operatorname{Ad}_{\zeta(y)}z)\). In Jin's
206: identification \((v,0)\sim(g(y)v,1)\), a connection is
207: \(A_\theta=-\theta\,dg\,g^{-1}\). With \(\omega=dg\,g^{-1}\),
208: \[
209:  F=-d\theta\,\omega+(\theta^2-\theta)\omega^2,\qquad
210:  \int_{S^3}\operatorname{tr}(\omega^3)
211:                       =-24\pi^2\deg g.
212: \]
213: The orientation is \(S^3\times S^1\), so direct integration gives
214: \(c_2=\deg g\) and \(p_1(\operatorname{ad}g)=-4\deg g\).
215: The vertical Euler class of this sphere bundle is \(2x\), and
216: the base \(S^3\times S^1\) has \(H^2=0\). Therefore
217: \[
218:                        (k_L,\ell_L)=(-1,-4).
219: \]
220: Composing the actual equivariant return with \(L^{-t}\) kills its
221: variation and gives
222: \[
223:                       k_0=0,\qquad \ell_0=\ell-4k.    \tag{K23.9}
224: \]
225: Additivity follows from the mapping-torus pair-of-pants bordism
226: carrying the actual Euler line.
227: 
228: Put \({\cal B}=LR^{-1}\). The relative class after (K23.9) is
229: \({\cal B}^b{\cal C}^s\). Its double is the actual principal circle
230: bundle over the closed conifold mapping torus. Its tangent bundle
